<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">20 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 20 May, from 325 to 1940.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">325 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,701 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Council of Nicaea opens under Constantine</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> from across the empire gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in the first ecumenical council of the Church. It condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> and produced the first version of the Nicene Creed.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1498 CE <span class="tih-ago">528 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vasco da Gama reaches Calicut in India</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Da Gama's fleet arrived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Malabar coast after sailing around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The sea route he opened allowed Portugal to bypass the overland spice trade and build an Asian empire.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1570 CE <span class="tih-ago">456 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ortelius publishes the first modern atlas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Abraham Ortelius issued the Theatrum Orbis Terrarum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, a bound collection of uniform maps covering the known world. It became a bestseller and went through many editions.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1802 CE <span class="tih-ago">224 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon restores slavery in French colonies</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A law of the Consulate maintained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> where it had not been abolished and paved the way for its return elsewhere, reversing the Revolution's abolition of 1794. Resistance helped drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> to independence.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1873 CE <span class="tih-ago">153 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Levi Strauss and Jacob Davis patent riveted denim trousers</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The patent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> rivets at the stress points of work trousers marked the birth of blue jeans. Made for labourers in the American West, they became a global garment.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1927 CE <span class="tih-ago">99 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles Lindbergh takes off for Paris</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lindbergh left Roosevelt Field, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in the Spirit of St Louis. He landed at Le Bourget 33.5 hours later, completing the first solo non-stop transatlantic flight.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1932 CE <span class="tih-ago">94 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Amelia Earhart sets off on her solo Atlantic flight</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Earhart took off from Harbour Grace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, and landed in a field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> the next day. She became the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1940 CE <span class="tih-ago">86 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">First prisoners are brought to Auschwitz</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Thirty German criminal prisoners arrived from Sachsenhausen to act as camp functionaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> grew into the largest Nazi death camp, where about 1.1 million people, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, were murdered.</p></div></li></ol>
